/* generated by scripts/build_pages.py from js/data.js: do not edit */
(function(D){D.articles=[{"slug":"competitor-analysis-small-business","cat":"SEO","title":"Competitor analysis for small businesses: a free method","date":"2026-11-16","body":"<p>Most small businesses never study their competitors in any structured way. They glance at a rival's Instagram, feel either smug or anxious, and go back to work. That is a loss, because a one-hour competitor analysis answers the three questions that decide most marketing outcomes: who are you really competing with, what are they doing that works, and where is the gap you can own. This guide is the exact method we use at FODO, and you can run most of it with free tools, including our own <a href=\"/competitor-finder/\">Competitor Finder</a>.</p>\n<h3>First, define who your competitors actually are</h3><p>Your competitor is not the business you dislike or the biggest brand in your category. It is whoever appears when your customer searches for what you sell, and whoever your customer shortlists next to you. Those are often different companies for different services, so make two lists:</p><ul><li><strong>Search competitors:</strong> open an incognito window and search the three phrases a customer would use, for example \"wedding photographer in Dwarka\" or \"corporate travel company Delhi\". Note every business on page one: organic results, the map pack, and the ads.</li><li><strong>Shortlist competitors:</strong> ask your last five customers who else they considered. Their answers are usually a surprise, and they matter more than any tool.</li></ul><p>Keep the combined list to five or six names. Analysis of twenty competitors never gets finished.</p>\n<h3>What to record for each competitor</h3><p>Make a simple sheet with one row per competitor and these columns. Everything here is public information:</p><ul><li><strong>Positioning:</strong> the first sentence on their homepage. Who do they say they serve, and what outcome do they promise? If you cannot tell, that is a finding too.</li><li><strong>Offer and pricing signals:</strong> packages, starting prices, free audits or trials, guarantees. Many businesses hide prices; note whether they do, because showing yours can become a differentiator.</li><li><strong>Search presence:</strong> do they rank organically for your money keywords, or do they buy ads, or both? Which pages rank: the homepage, a service page, or a blog article?</li><li><strong>Google Business Profile:</strong> rating, number of reviews, how recently a customer reviewed them, whether the owner replies. Review velocity is one of the most honest signals of how a local business is really doing.</li><li><strong>Social proof and content:</strong> where are they active, how often do they post, what format earns them real comments rather than polite likes?</li><li><strong>Speed and experience:</strong> open their site on your phone on mobile data. Slow, cluttered pages are an open goal.</li></ul>\n<h3>Run the same checks on your own business</h3><p>This is the step people skip. Fill in the same row for yourself, honestly, before you read the comparison. The gaps become obvious within minutes: perhaps every competitor shows prices and you do not, or you have the best reviews but the slowest website, or nobody in the market writes anything useful, which means the first business that does will own the informational searches.</p>\n<h3>Turn observations into three decisions</h3><p>A competitor analysis is only useful if it ends in decisions. We force every analysis into exactly three:</p><ul><li><strong>One thing to neutralise.</strong> The competitor advantage that is costing you customers right now, for example their 120 reviews against your 9. You rarely need to beat it this quarter; you need to stop losing on it. A steady review-request habit closes most of that gap in months.</li><li><strong>One gap to own.</strong> Something customers care about that nobody in your list does well: fast quotes, transparent pricing, content in the customer's own language, a genuinely useful free tool. This becomes a line on your homepage, not a secret strategy document.</li><li><strong>One thing to stop copying.</strong> Something you imitated because everyone does it, that the analysis shows is not working for anyone, most often posting daily to a social channel where no competitor gets any engagement either.</li></ul>\n<h3>The free-tool stack for a one-hour analysis</h3><p>You do not need paid software for a first pass:</p><ul><li><strong><a href=\"/competitor-finder/\">FODO Competitor Finder</a></strong>: enter your website and it finds the businesses competing with you in search and compares your presence against theirs. It is free and takes a few minutes.</li><li><strong>Incognito Google search</strong> for the shortlist and the map pack.</li><li><strong>Google Business Profile listings</strong> for ratings, reviews and reply behaviour.</li><li><strong>Meta Ad Library</strong> to see which competitors are running Facebook and Instagram ads right now, and what they are saying.</li><li><strong>PageSpeed Insights</strong> to compare site speed on mobile.</li></ul><p>When you outgrow the free pass, a deeper audit like our <a href=\"/business-x-ray/\">Business X-Ray</a> reads your site, your market and your competitors together and turns the findings into a 30-day plan with evidence for each item.</p>\n<h3>How often to repeat it</h3><p>Quarterly is enough for most small businesses. Put a recurring reminder in your calendar, reuse the same sheet, and add one column per quarter so you can see movement: who climbed into the map pack, whose reviews stalled, who started advertising. Trends across two or three quarters tell you far more than any single snapshot.</p>\n<h3>Common mistakes to avoid</h3><ul><li><strong>Copying the market leader.</strong> Their playbook depends on budgets and brand recognition you do not have yet. Copy the direction of insurgents, not the tactics of incumbents.</li><li><strong>Confusing activity with results.</strong> A competitor posting daily may be getting nothing from it. Look for engagement, reviews and rankings, not effort.</li><li><strong>Analysis without a deadline.</strong> Book one hour, finish the sheet, make the three decisions, stop. The value is in the decisions, not the document.</li><li><strong>Ignoring indirect competition.</strong> For many services your real competitor is the customer doing nothing, or doing it themselves. Your marketing has to beat that option too.</li></ul>\n<h3>The takeaway</h3><p>Competitor analysis for a small business is not espionage and it is not a 40-page report. It is one honest hour: list five or six real competitors, record the same public facts about each of them and about yourself, then commit to one thing to neutralise, one gap to own and one habit to stop. Run it quarterly and your marketing decisions start compounding, because they are based on the market you are actually in rather than the one you assume. If you want the first pass done for you, run the free <a href=\"/competitor-finder/\">Competitor Finder</a> or <a href=\"/contact/\">talk to FODO</a> about a full growth audit.</p>"},{"slug":"traffic-vs-customers","cat":"Growth","title":"Why more traffic does not automatically mean more customers","date":"2026-10-01","body":"<p>Traffic is the easiest marketing number to grow and the easiest to misread. Buy more clicks, publish more posts, and the sessions chart goes up. The bank balance often does not follow. Understanding why is the first step to spending more intelligently.</p>\n<h3>Traffic is a cost until it converts</h3><p>Every visit costs something, money for ads, time for content, attention from your team. A visit only becomes valuable when it does something: enquires, buys, signs up, or returns later. If you measure traffic without measuring what traffic does, you are measuring spend and calling it progress.</p>\n<h3>Three reasons traffic grows while customers do not</h3><p><strong>Wrong people.</strong> Broad targeting and curiosity-driven content attract visitors who were never going to buy. The sessions are real; the intent is not.</p><p><strong>Broken path.</strong> The visitor was right but the page was wrong: slow, unclear, no obvious next step, a form that asks too much. Illustratively, if a page converts 1 in 100 visitors, doubling traffic doubles the cost of every leak too.</p><p><strong>No follow-up.</strong> Many customers do not buy on the first visit. Without email, WhatsApp or retargeting, each visit is a one-time event.</p>\n<h3>What to measure instead</h3><ul><li>Conversion rate by source, which channels bring people who act?</li><li>Cost per lead or acquisition, not cost per click.</li><li>Lead quality, agreed with the people who talk to leads.</li><li>Return visits and repeat purchases.</li></ul>\n<h3>A practical check</h3><p>Pick your top three traffic sources. For each, write down the conversion rate and the cost per conversion. If one source has high traffic and low conversion, decide: fix the page it lands on, or stop paying for it. That one exercise usually changes next month's budget more than any new channel would.</p>"},{"slug":"diagnose-landing-page","cat":"Paid Media","title":"How to diagnose a landing page before increasing ad spend","date":"2026-09-24","body":"<p>Scaling spend on a leaking page is the most common way to lose money in paid media. Before you raise a budget, spend thirty minutes on this diagnosis.</p>\n<h3>1. Message match</h3><p>Open your ad and your landing page side by side. Does the headline on the page repeat the promise in the ad? If the ad says \"free assessment\" and the page says \"welcome to our company\", visitors feel lost and leave.</p>\n<h3>2. One job</h3><p>Count the calls to action. If there are more than one (plus a small secondary), the page is asking the visitor to make decisions you should have made for them.</p>\n<h3>3. Above-the-fold clarity</h3><p>Without scrolling, can a stranger answer three questions: what is this, who is it for, what do I do next? Read it on a phone, not a monitor.</p>\n<h3>4. Speed</h3><p>Load the page on mobile data. If you notice the wait, your visitors are already gone. Compress images, remove unused scripts, and avoid heavy embeds above the fold.</p>\n<h3>5. Form friction</h3><p>Every field should earn its place. Ask what you need to follow up well, usually name, one contact method, and one qualifying question. Add the rest later in conversation.</p>\n<h3>6. Trust that is true</h3><p>Specific, honest proof beats generic badges. If you do not have testimonials yet, say what you do and how you work. Never invent proof; it is both risky and obvious.</p>\n<h3>7. Tracking</h3><p>Submit a test enquiry. Did it appear in your analytics as a conversion? Did someone receive it? If either answer is no, fix tracking before spending another rupee.</p>\n<p>If the page passes all seven, increase the budget in steps and watch cost per conversion, not just volume. If it fails any, fix that first, it is almost always cheaper than buying more traffic.</p>"},{"slug":"small-business-seo-foundation","cat":"SEO","title":"A practical small-business SEO foundation","date":"2026-09-17","body":"<p>SEO advice is often written for large sites with large teams. A small business needs a foundation it can actually maintain. Here is one.</p>\n<h3>Get the technical basics right once</h3><ul><li>One version of your site (www or non-www, HTTPS) with the other redirecting.</li><li>Every important page reachable within two or three clicks from the homepage.</li><li>Fast pages, compressed images, no bloated plugins.</li><li>A sitemap submitted in Google Search Console.</li></ul>\n<h3>One page per thing you sell</h3><p>A single \"services\" page that lists everything rarely ranks for anything. Give each core service its own page that explains the problem, the process, who it is for, and how to enquire. Write for a person, not for a keyword list.</p>\n<h3>Match the words people use</h3><p>Look at how customers describe their problem in emails and calls. Those phrases belong in your headings. Search Console will show you which queries already bring impressions, start there.</p>\n<h3>Local signals if you are local</h3><p>Keep your Google Business Profile accurate and active, make sure your address and phone match everywhere, and ask real customers for reviews with a simple, consistent process.</p>\n<h3>Internal links are free</h3><p>Link from articles to service pages and from service pages to related services. Use descriptive anchor text. This helps visitors and search engines understand what matters.</p>\n<h3>Publish less, but useful</h3><p>One well-answered question a month beats four thin posts. Answer what customers actually ask before they buy.</p>\n<h3>Measure what matters</h3><p>Track enquiries and calls from organic search, not just sessions. SEO takes months; the measure of progress is more of the right visitors doing the right thing.</p>"},{"slug":"social-content-pillars","cat":"Social","title":"How to choose social content pillars that support business goals","date":"2026-09-10","body":"<p>Content pillars are the three to five themes your social presence keeps returning to. Chosen well, they make planning easier and make your brand recognisable. Chosen badly, they are just categories for random posts.</p>\n<h3>Start from the goal, not the platform</h3><p>Write down what social media should cause for the business: enquiries, bookings, store visits, trust before a sales call. Every pillar should be able to answer \"how does this help that happen?\"</p>\n<h3>A simple pillar structure</h3><ul><li><strong>Proof</strong>, how you work, behind the scenes, process, results you can honestly show.</li><li><strong>Teach</strong>, answers to the questions customers ask before buying.</li><li><strong>Point of view</strong>, what you believe about your field that others do not say.</li><li><strong>Offer</strong>, what you sell and how to start, stated clearly and regularly.</li></ul>\n<h3>Test pillars against reality</h3><p>Can your team produce this content every week without heroics? If a pillar needs a video crew you do not have, it will die in month two. Illustratively, a founder who can record one voice note a day has a sustainable pillar; one who needs a studio does not.</p>\n<h3>Give each pillar a job and a metric</h3><p>Proof builds trust (saves, profile visits). Teach builds reach (shares). Point of view builds memory (comments). Offer builds action (link clicks, messages). Review monthly which pillar is doing its job.</p>\n<h3>Rotate, do not ration</h3><p>You do not need equal volume. Lean toward what works for your goals while keeping the others alive so the feed does not become a sales page.</p>"},{"slug":"useful-marketing-dashboard","cat":"Strategy","title":"What a useful marketing dashboard should answer","date":"2026-09-03","body":"<p>Most dashboards are built by asking \"what data do we have?\" Useful ones are built by asking \"what decisions do we make?\" Here is how to design one that gets opened.</p>\n<h3>Start with five questions</h3><ol><li>Are we getting enough of the right enquiries or sales this month?</li><li>What does each one cost, by channel?</li><li>Where in the funnel are we losing people?</li><li>Which content or creative is working right now?</li><li>Is anything broken, tracking, forms, spend pacing?</li></ol>\n<h3>One number per question, then detail</h3><p>The top row should answer each question at a glance. Trends and breakdowns live below. If a visitor needs to scroll to know whether the month is on track, the dashboard has failed.</p>\n<h3>Agree definitions first</h3><p>What counts as a lead? A form submission, a qualified call, a booked meeting? Write it down. Illustratively, two teams reporting \"leads\" with different definitions will argue about numbers instead of acting on them.</p>\n<h3>Compare against something</h3><p>A number alone means nothing. Show the previous period and the agreed target. Colour only when it changes a decision.</p>\n<h3>Trust it or fix it</h3><p>Add a small \"data health\" section: last tracking QA date, known gaps, platform discrepancies. A dashboard that admits its limits is more useful than one that hides them.</p>\n<h3>Keep it short</h3><p>If it needs a tour, it is a report, not a dashboard. Monthly deep-dives belong in a separate document with commentary.</p>"},{"slug":"first-paid-media-experiment","cat":"Paid Media","title":"How to structure a first paid-media experiment","date":"2026-08-27","body":"<p>A first campaign should be designed to teach you something, not to \"go viral\". Here is a structure that keeps the lesson clear and the cost controlled.</p>\n<h3>Choose one objective</h3><p>Leads, purchases or bookings, one. Mixed objectives make every result ambiguous.</p>\n<h3>Choose one audience and one page</h3><p>Pick the audience you understand best and send them to a page built for this offer. Changing audience, creative and page at once means you will never know what mattered.</p>\n<h3>Write a hypothesis</h3><p>\"We believe [audience] will respond to [message] because [reason]. We will know within [time/budget] if cost per [result] is under [threshold].\" Illustrative thresholds are fine, the point is deciding in advance what success looks like.</p>\n<h3>Test creative, not everything</h3><p>Run three to five creative angles against the same audience. Hooks and formats are where early learning is fastest and cheapest.</p>\n<h3>Set a learning budget</h3><p>Decide how much you are willing to spend to learn, and stop there to review. Do not let the platform's \"learning phase\" become your budgeting strategy.</p>\n<h3>Verify tracking before launch</h3><p>Fire a test conversion and see it land in your analytics and your inbox. Nothing is more expensive than a week of unmeasured spend.</p>\n<h3>Review with evidence</h3><p>At the end, answer: which creative won, what did a result cost, what surprised us, and what is the next single change? That answer is worth more than the first campaign's results.</p>"},{"slug":"positioning-and-conversion","cat":"Brand","title":"How brand positioning affects conversion","date":"2026-08-20","body":"<p>Positioning sounds like a branding exercise. It is actually a conversion lever. The clearer a visitor is about what you are and who you are for, the faster they decide.</p>\n<h3>Confusion is friction</h3><p>When a page tries to appeal to everyone, each visitor has to work out whether it applies to them. Most will not bother. A clear statement: \"for growing D2C brands who have outgrown boosted posts\", lets the right people lean in and the wrong people leave quickly, which also improves your ad metrics.</p>\n<h3>Specific beats superior</h3><p>\"Best digital agency\" is a claim; \"we fix funnels before scaling ads\" is a position. Claims invite doubt. Positions invite the question \"is that my problem?\", a much better conversation to start.</p>\n<h3>Positioning shapes the whole page</h3><p>Once the position is clear, headlines, proof, objections and calls to action write themselves. Without it, every section argues with the next.</p>\n<h3>Price and position must agree</h3><p>Premium pricing with discount-shop language, or value pricing with luxury visuals, creates a mismatch the visitor feels before they can explain it.</p>\n<h3>A simple test</h3><p>Show your homepage to someone outside your business for ten seconds and ask them to describe who it is for and what it does. If their answer is vague, the problem is positioning, not design.</p>"},{"slug":"simple-lead-follow-up-system","cat":"Growth","title":"A simple follow-up system for inbound leads","date":"2026-08-13","body":"<p>Most businesses do not have a lead problem; they have a follow-up problem. Enquiries arrive, sit in an inbox, and go cold. This system takes an afternoon to set up.</p>\n<h3>1. One inbox, one owner</h3><p>Route every enquiry, forms, WhatsApp, email, calls, into one place (a CRM, a shared sheet, or a labelled inbox). Name one person responsible each day.</p>\n<h3>2. Acknowledge in minutes</h3><p>Automate a short, honest reply: what you received, what happens next, when they will hear from a human. On WhatsApp this is a template; on email it is an autoresponder.</p>\n<h3>3. Qualify with two questions</h3><p>Ask what they want to achieve and when. Those two answers tell you how to prioritise and how to prepare.</p>\n<h3>4. A five-touch sequence</h3><p>Day 0 reply, day 1 call or message, day 3 useful resource, day 7 check-in, day 14 final friendly close. Write the messages once; personalise the first line each time.</p>\n<h3>5. Record outcomes</h3><p>Won, lost, no response, not a fit, and why. Within a month you will see patterns: which sources bring fit, which messages get replies.</p>\n<h3>6. Review weekly</h3><p>Fifteen minutes: how many enquiries, how fast the first response, how many moved forward. Speed to first response is usually the number that moves everything else.</p>\n<p>Automation can run steps 2 and 4; humans must own steps 3 and 5. Done consistently, this system often matters more than the next campaign.</p>"}];})(window.FODO_DATA=window.FODO_DATA||{});
